{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"driveable-glandulous-zeren","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## What happens\n\n`src/rebar/llm/workflows/review_skeleton.yaml` names reviewer prompts by id. Nothing\nchecks that those ids resolve against the packaged catalog in\n`src/rebar/llm/reviewers/index.json`. A step naming a prompt that was renamed or\ndeleted stays in the file and fails only when the workflow runs.\n\nFound during epic 87d8, which retired the `ticket-quality` reviewer. The workflow\nreference had to be caught by hand-censusing the tree per symbol; no gate would have\ncaught it. A sampling census missed a comparable reference in\n`docs/research/task-decomposition-sota-2026.md`, which is the same failure mode in a\ndifferent file.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe packaged catalog is a contract, and reviewer ids are the only handle onto it.\nDeleting or renaming a reviewer is a routine maintenance operation; today its blast\nradius is found by grep discipline rather than by a check, so it is found reliably only\nwhen someone remembers to sweep the whole tree instead of the files they expect.\n\nA dangling reference also survives review: the diff that deletes a prompt does not show\nthe file that still names it.\n\n## Change\n\nAdd a test that loads every workflow YAML shipped under `src/rebar/llm/workflows/`,\ncollects the reviewer ids its steps name, and asserts each resolves against the\npackaged catalog.\n\nDriving it off the shipped workflow directory rather than a hardcoded file list means a\nnew workflow is covered the day it is added.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] **AC1** A test collects reviewer ids from every `*.yaml` under\n      `src/rebar/llm/workflows/` and asserts each resolves against\n      `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/index.json`.\n- [ ] **AC2** The test discovers workflow files by globbing that directory, not from a\n      hardcoded list, so a newly added workflow is covered without editing the test.\n- [ ] **AC3** Mutating a reviewer id in a workflow fixture to a name absent from the\n      catalog makes the test fail, and the failure message names both the offending id\n      and the file that holds it.\n- [ ] **AC4** The test passes on the tree as it stands.\n- [ ] **AC5** The test does not require network access or an LLM.\n- [ ] **AC6** `make verify` exits 0.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nValidating prompt references in `docs/`. Documentation prose legitimately names\nretired ids in historical passages, and `docs/release-notes.md` names them by design,\nso a docs check needs a different rule and belongs on its own ticket.","id":"bc30-a85f-1712-43f3","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Nothing checks that workflow YAML reviewer ids resolve"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793376529214522960,"uuid":"13a8b1ec-4a97-47bc-9532-8e465a5efe34"}